\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  Likely Stand Pat on Policy Rate -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,174 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2023\par 05:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0337 GMT - \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may keep its benchmark seven-day reverse repo rate unchanged at 5.75% at its August meeting, according to six economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. As the Indonesian rupiah is under pressure due to a rising dollar, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  may continue to focus on enhancing rupiah stability, ANZ Research says in a note. With inflation easing and CPI expected to remain benign, an interest-rate hike is unlikely to be \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's preferred policy response, it adds. ANZ expects \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue stabilizing the rupiah via market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The policy decision is due Thursday. (yingxian.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT - Australia's property market is running hot despite a massive jump in mortgage interest rates in the last year, with successful sales at auction over the weekend reaching their highest level in six weeks. Property research group \uc2 Corelogic\uc2  says 2,056 properties went to auction on Saturday through to Sunday, with 74% achieving a sale. Kaytlin Ezzy, economist at \uc2 CoreLogic\uc2 , says it was the busiest auction week since before Easter and the fifth busiest week in the year to date. The increase in auction numbers was met with an increase in buyer activity, with six of the seven capitals recording a week-on-week increase in sales. In the same week last year, there were 1,816 auctions held, with 58% sold. (james.glynn@wsj.com;@JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0232 GMT - China's economic growth may face further\u65279\'FE\'FF pressure as the post-pandemic pent-up demand for travel runs its course, \uc2 Nomura\uc2  analysts say in a note. They cut 3Q and 4Q on-year GDP growth forecasts to 3.7% and 4.0%, respectively, from the earlier forecast of 4.9% for each quarter, following the weaker-than-expected July data. While Beijing's 2023 GDP growth target at around 5.0% is still achievable, the weakening momentum, timid policy response and worsening market confidence raise the risk of growth being below 5%, they say. \uc2 Nomura\uc2  trims its 2023 GDP growth forecast to 4.6% from 5.1% previously. "At some point of time in H2, we expect Beijing to significantly step up its policy support to arrest the downward spiral," they add. (monica.gupta@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT - Chinese yuan is weaker against USD in the onshore and offshore markets in the morning Asian session, pressured by China's one-year loan prime rate cut. It still seems evident over the past two weeks that China's government seems unwilling to conduct substantial fiscal stimulus to turn the economy around decisively, \uc2 Alvin T. Tan\uc2 , head of Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategy at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  says in an email. While there are arguable long-term merits to China's fiscal conservatism, near-term implications remain negative for the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and asset markets, on top of climbing Treasury yields, Tan adds. USD/CNY rises 0.3% to 7.3057, USD/CNH adds 0.2% to 7.3203. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT - \uc2 UBS\uc2  lowers its China 2023 GDP growth forecast to 4.8% from 5.2% previously, due to a weaker property sector and softer external demand. The investment bank also lowers its China 2024 GDP growth forecast to 4.2% from 5.0% previously. "The government's policy support has arguably been less than was indicated earlier in the year, and less than we expected," it says in a note to clients.(singaporeeditors@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT - Malaysia's 2023 GDP could grow 4.0%, compared with CGS-CIMB's previous estimate of a 4.6% expansion, due to weaker-than-expected 2Q GDP and ongoing external headwinds, economists Nazmi Idrus and Mas Aida Che Mansor say in a note. Although domestic growth may remain resilient, they think that GDP's performance is still anchored to the external demand outlook and key export markets have been subdued thus far. July's trade data and 2Q GDP hint that there is a potential weak external environment in 3Q, they add. As 2Q GDP weakened, CGS-CIMB maintains its expectation that \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  will keep its overnight policy rate unchanged at 3.0% for the rest of 2023. (yingxian.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0118 GMT - The New Zealand government will publish the pre-election economic and fiscal update on Sept. 12, and given the weakness of the economy, it is expected to project a bigger budget deficit for 2023-24, says Darren Gibbs, senior economist at \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . The outlook for nominal GDP, which drives the tax base, has deteriorated since the last update, and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  now expects a 2023-24 budget deficit of NZ$12.0 billion, up from the recent budget projection of a deficit of NZ$7.6 billion. A substantial deterioration is also likely to be shown for 2024-25, it adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT - The New Zealand economy is expected to be flat until early 2024, says \uc2 Nick Tuffley\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 ASB\uc2 . As such, the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  looks set to hold the OCR steady at 5.50% until 2H 2024 before making cuts. For the moment, net immigration is up and providing a boost to the labor market, with inflation expected to remain above 5% for the rest of 2023, he adds. Despite the soft short-term outlook, there are early indications some things may be nearing a turning point, particularly in the housing market. It looks like that market is stabilizing and prices will start creeping back up in the coming year, Tuffley says. (james.glynn@wsj.com ; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0023 GMT - South Korea's Kospi is 0.4% higher at 2514.66 in early trade, led by biotech and internet stocks. Still, trading volume remains thin, as traders stay cautious ahead of \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2 's rate-setting meeting due Thursday. Investors will also likely focus on Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 's speech due Friday at the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's annual symposium in Jackson Hole. USD/KRW is 0.1% higher at 1,340.10. Vaccine maker \uc2 SK Bioscience\uc2  rises 2.2% as its sibling company \uc2 SK Biopharmaceuticals\uc2  gains 2.7%. Internet platform giants \uc2 Naver\uc2  and \uc2 Kakao Corp\uc2 . add 1.7% and 1.6%, respectively. Index heavyweight \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  is up 0.3%.(kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2345 GMT - Japanese stocks may be rangebound as uncertainty continues about the pace of policy tightening by central banks and its economic impact. Nikkei futures open up 40 points at 31490 on the SGX. Investors are focusing on the Jackson Hole symposium later this week. USD/JPY is at 145.33, compared with 145.36 as of Friday's Tokyo stock market close. Some financial stocks may underperform following drops in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields on Friday. The Nikkei Stock Average fell 0.6% to 31450.76 on Friday.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 20, 2023 23:37 ET (03:37 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230821ej8l0007d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
