\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Indonesian rupiah dives as Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  slip on US rate hike bets\b0\par\par\uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2023\par 06:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par         * \par       Indonesian rupiah hits over 3-month low\par     \par \par         * \par       U.S. 2-year Treasury yield hits highest since June 2007\par     \par \par         * \par       Shares in Philippines, South Korea among top losers\par     \par \par         * \par       Data from Taiwan, Malaysia and Singapore awaited  \par     \par \par   \par     By Nausheen Thusoo\par        July 7 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah weakened to its\par lowest level in more than three months on Friday, leading Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses, as the U.S. dollar remained firm on persistent\par bets the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  would keep interest rates higher for\par longer. \par     Regional equities dived across the board as resilient U.S.\par jobs data sparked a sell-off in U.S. Treasuries, which in turn\par reverberated in bond markets across Asia on strengthening\par expectations the Fed was not yet done with its rate hiking\par cycle.\par     "We still cannot rule out potential for a USD pullback in\par the near-term but the higher for longer narrative could continue\par to keep the USD-Asia pairings buoyant on dips," Maybank analysts\par said in a note.\par     Elevated Treasury yields could continue to buoy U.S.-Asia\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs unless U.S. non-farm payrolls data surprises to\par the downside later on Friday, the analysts added.\par     The dollar held tight ranges after strong jobs data,\par which triggered a Treasuries selloff into overdrive. The\par two-year Treasury yield, which typically moves in\par step with interest rate expectations, was up 5.1 basis points at\par 5.002% after rising to 5.120%, the highest since June 2007.\par     However, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  said most emerging market assets had\par historically reacted positively to U.S. economic resilience and\par the trajectories of central banks in those countries were\par unlikely be derailed by the Fed.\par     In Asia, the Indonesian rupiah depreciated 0.6% to\par hit its lowest level since March 27 as investors exited on risks\par related to higher U.S. interest rates.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a statement said it was ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to defend the rupiah when needed.\par     The Thai baht, the Taiwanese dollar and\par Malaysian ringgit were down 0.2%, while the Philippine\par peso firmed 0.2%.\par     Market participants were bracing for a slew of data later on\par Friday, including Singapore and Malaysia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves and Taiwan's trade data. \par     "Trade figures for June in Taiwan could show rates of\par decline moderating in year-on-year terms, though they are likely\par to remain in a double-digit descent," ING Markets said in a\par note.\par     The South Korean won also skidded 0.6%. The\par country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is scheduled to announce an interest rate\par decision next week.\par     Equities in the region were largely in the red, with shares\par in the Philippines losing 1.1% to hit lowest in two\par weeks, while those in Singapore, Malaysia, and\par Indonesia fell around 0.3%. \par     \par     HIGHLIGHTS:    \par     ** Indonesia's benchmark 10-year yield jumps 4.4\par basis points to 6.254%\par     ** Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves drop to $137.5 bln at June-end\par     ** India's 10-year benchmark yield jumps to\par 7.165%, highest since April 21\par     ** U.S. 10-year Treasury yield at 4.0396%,\par highest since early March\par     \par  Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                         \par  at 0315 GMT                                        \par  COUNTRY   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC          \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   INDE  STOCKS  STOCKS\par                       DAILY %  YTD %     X   DAILY   YTD %\par                                                  %  \par  Japan                  +0.10  -8.89  <.N2  -0.47   25.00\par                                       25>           \par  China     <CNY=CFXS    +0.05  -4.76  <.SS   -0.38    3.37\par            >                          EC>           \par  India                   0.00  +0.25  <.NS    0.00    7.69\par                                       EI>           \par  Indonesi               -0.63  +2.84  <.JK   -0.19   -1.55\par  a                                    SE>           \par  Malaysia               -0.17  -5.74  <.KL   -0.27   -7.57\par                                       SE>           \par  Philippi               +0.21  +0.22  <.PS   -0.85   -2.24\par  nes                                  I>            \par  S.Korea   <KRW=KFTC    -0.56  -3.34  <.KS   -1.33   12.79\par            >                          11>           \par  Singapor               +0.04  -0.98  <.ST   -0.39   -3.48\par  e                                    I>            \par  Taiwan                 -0.22  -1.86  <.TW   -0.52   17.95\par                                       II>           \par  Thailand               -0.17  -1.83  <.SE   -0.52  -11.14\par                                       TI>   