\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  hit by rising global rates, recession risks\b0\par\par\uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2022\par 07:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par         * \par       South Korean won, shares lead losses\par     \par \par         * \par       Thai baht lingers at over 16-yr low\par     \par \par         * \par       \f28 \uc2 \f2 BoT\f28 \uc2 \f2  expected to deliver 25 bps rate hike - Reuters poll\par     \par \par   \par     By Upasana Singh\par        Sept 28 (Reuters) - Worldwide ructions in financial\par markets swept up Asian emerging economies, with most of their\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down on Wednesday as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's ever\par more hawkish policy tightening stance to fight inflation raised\par global recession risks.\par     As many developed economies grappled with the prospects of\par further rate hikes, and the sterling remained under fire due to\par large unfunded UK tax cut plans, the dollar strode to near a\par two-decade high, leaving the likes of the Thai baht struggling\par at more than 16-year lows.\par     The rising borrowing costs globally have added to pressure\par across emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , raising the risk of larger\par hikes in these countries to fight inflation and reduce the \par premium offered by dollar-based assets. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Thailand (BOT) \f28 \uc2 \f2 is expected to deliver another 25\par basis point rate hike later in the day - even as many of its\par peers opt for larger increases to fight rising prices, a Reuters\par poll showed.\par     "The ongoing market adjustment for a more aggressive Fed's\par policy outlook continues to weigh on the region," said Yeap Jun\par Rong, market strategist at IG.\par     The Thai baht, which has lost nearly 12% this year,\par fell 0.3% and lingered at its lowest level since July 2006.\par     Inflation in Southeast Asia's second-largest economy jumped\par to a 14-year high in August, and a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par exacerbated the price pressures by raising the cost of imports.\par Stocks in Bangkok dropped 0.8%.\par     Elsewhere, Singapore's dollar and Malaysia's ringgit\par  weakened 0.5% and 0.2%, respectively, while South Korea's\par won led losses in the region as it depreciated 1.2%\par to hit a more than 13-year low.\par     The broad rally in the U.S. dollar meant few of its rivals\par escaped unscathed. The dollar index gained 0.4% to\par 114.56, near to its top of 114.58 hit on Monday.\par     Indonesia's rupiah, among the best performing\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Southeast Asia, weakened 0.7%, while equities in\par Jakarta were flat.\par     "We expect USDIDR to climb to fresh multi-year highs,\par reflecting near-term headwinds from broad dollar strength and a\par deteriorating current account position over the medium term,"\par analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a note.\par     However, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has continued with its "triple\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " to guard against excessive falls in the rupiah\par exchange rate, an official said.    \par     Over in China, the yuan slipped 0.6%, the weakest\par since the global financial crisis in 2008, even as the country's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday announced fresh steps to slow the pace of\par the yuan's recent depreciation by making it more expensive to\par bet against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The Philippine peso was the only bright spot among\par the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as it managed to eke out a 0.1% gain.\par However, the country's benchmark index dropped 1.2%.\par     In stock markets, equities in Seoul slipped 2.8% and\par led declines. Stocks in Singapore and Kuala Lumpur\par  retreated 1.3% and 0.5%, respectively.\par     HIGHLIGHTS:\par     ** Thai govt plans $21.6 bln in new borrowing in 2023 fiscal\par year\par     ** Indonesia, Malaysia c.banks renew local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap\par arrangement\par     ** China prepares to tweak yuan fixing process to slow its\par fall - source\par     The following table shows rates for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against\par the dollar at 0358 GMT.\par  COUNTRY   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC          \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   INDE  STOCKS  STOCKS\par                       DAILY %  YTD %     X   DAILY   YTD %\par                                                  %  \par  Japan                  +0.06  -20.4  <.N2  -2.25   -9.63\par                                    5  25>           \par  China     <CNY=CFXS    -0.60  -12.0  <.SS   -0.75  -15.64\par            >                       2  EC>           \par  India                  -0.37  -9.22  <.NS   -0.80   -2.79\par                                       EI>           \par  Indonesi               -0.72  -6.43  <.JK    0.01    8.08\par  a                                    SE>           \par  Malaysia               -0.17  -9.81  <.KL   -0.52  -10.46\par                                       SE>           \par  Philippi               +0.14  -13.5  <.PS   -1.24  -16.53\par  nes                               7  I>            \par  S.Korea   <KRW=KFTC    -1.22  -17.3  <.KS   -2.