\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-As Fed taper looms, global central banks eye their own exits from stimulus\b0\par\par\uc2 1,019 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2021\par 11:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story from earlier in the day with no changes to text)\par \par \uc2 * Canada, Norway among central banks creeping toward exit\par \par \uc2 * Emerging central banks most vulnerable to Fed taper fallout\par \par \uc2 * Asian financial crisis may offer lessons - or not\par \par \uc2 * All eyes on Fed's possible taper signals at Jackson Hole\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara, Mfuneko Toyana and Karin Strohecker\par \par \uc2 TOKYO/JOHANNESBURG/LONDON, June 10 (Reuters) - Haunted by memories of past U.S. interest rate hikes, the world's central banks are laying the groundwork for a transition to life with less global stimulus, with many countries already signalling moves to the exit.\par \par \uc2 While the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is publicly committed to keeping interest rates near zero -- and no hikes are priced in until late next year at the earliest -- official comments about inflationary pressures could become a chorus in months ahead, making tapering a more concrete prospect and likely heightening volatility in global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 For some developed economies, a return to pre-pandemic conditions means \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stimulus withdrawal is already in the works.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, more vulnerable central banks are fortifying their financial systems to ward off the type of capital flight that hit emerging markets during the 2013 "taper tantrum," which was triggered by mere hints of Fed tightening after years of super-easy policy deployed during the Global Financial Crisis.\par \par \uc2 "There's a huge divergence among economies emerging from the pandemic, and those lagging behind. Some emerging central banks may be forced to raise rates to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even at the cost of hurting their still-fragile economies," said \uc2 Takahide Kiuchi\uc2 , a former \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (BOJ) board member.\par \par \uc2 "This trend may broaden if the Fed communicates its taper strategy in coming months. This could be among risks for the global economy," said Kiuchi, currently an economist at \uc2 Nomura Research Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Fed has said it would not start scaling back its huge stimulus until there had been "substantial further progress" in healing the U.S. job market.\par \par \uc2 While job recovery remains patch, stronger-than-expected inflation raises the possibility that the Fed may have to tighten policy sooner than expected.\par \par \uc2 For now, markets are bracing for the chance the Fed will start to communicate its taper strategy at its Jackson Hole symposium in August, with possible action later in the year.\par \par \uc2 Some central banks are already responding.\par \par \uc2 In April, \uc2 Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  became the first among \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  nations to withdraw its pandemic era stimulus and signalled rates could begin to rise in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already announced plans to raise rates in the third or fourth quarter of 2021.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand and South Korea have similarly dropped loud hints that policy tightening is on the agenda as conditions improve.\par \par \uc2 While decisions in such countries would primarily be driven by domestic considerations, the Fed's eventual withdrawal of support looms large as a global risk for every \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has hardly budged from its ultra-accommodative settings through decades of global cycles, may see an opportunity to dial back stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "A Fed rate hike may give the BOJ a perfect opportunity to normalise policy, without worrying too much about triggering a yen spike," Nomura's Kiuchi said.\par \par \uc2 WOBBLE WATCH\par \par \uc2 Developing economies face the biggest risks from Fed tightening, which have in the past caused market gyrations as rising U.S. interest rates attracted funds into dollar assets and away from emerging markets, as happened in the 1998 and 2013.\par \par \uc2 Asian markets, the epicentre of the 1998 Asian financial crisis, remain in significantly better shape with strong foreign reserves to support any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rout.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor last week said its reserves now exceed $600 billion, which it expects will help against challenges from "global spillovers."\par \par \uc2 But some analysts warn that lessons from Asia's financial crisis may not apply to the current pandemic-induced shock.\par \par \uc2 "This crisis is like no other in that it's not a financial crisis or an economic crisis," said former BOJ official Nobuyasu Atago, who is now an economist at Japan's \uc2 Ichiyoshi Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The unevenness of the current global economy creates various risks for emerging economies."\par \par \uc2 Among nations looking warily at the Fed is Indonesia, which relies on overseas inflows to fund its current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 "Next year we must prepare for possibilities of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Fed, to shift its monetary policy, reducing its liquidity \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Perry Warjiyo\uc2  said last month, adding such U.S. policy shifts would likely impact local bond yields.\par \par \uc2 The more vulnerable emerging market central banks, such as Brazil, Ghana and Armenia, have already started their tightening cycle on rising inflation pressures.\par \par \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to raise rates on Friday for the third time in a row with inflation well above its target, according to a Reuters poll.\par \par \uc2 Turkey was ahead of the pack and aggressively tightened last year, a move its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor hopes will serve as a "shield" against any Fed pivot.\par \par \uc2 But heavy foreign debt keeps Turkey vulnerable to talk of Fed tapering. Rising U.S. yields recently helped push the lira to all-time lows, delaying planned rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 For now, policymakers in places such as Thailand, the Philippines and South Africa buy the view the Fed won't hike prematurely and are confident that its communication to markets will be transparent.\par \par \uc2 But they acknowledge risks.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed has said it would like to see more inflation in the United States, they've indicated they'd have some tolerance for inflation," South African Reserve Bank Deputy Governor Fundi Tshazibana told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "What all of us don't know is what that tolerance band is."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara in Tokyo, Mfuneko Toyana in Johannesburg, Karin Strohecker in London; Additional reporting by Jonathan Spicer in Istanbul, Simon Johnson in Stockholm, Swati Bhat in Mumbai, Gwladys Fouche in Oslo; Writing by Sam Holmes and Leika Kihara; Editing by Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-6-10T10:00:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210610eh6a01iv1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
