\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Indonesia's rupiah at near 5-mth low as US bond yields rise\b0\par\par\uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2021\par 06:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Indonesia, Malaysia shares down around 1.5%\par     * U.S. dollar on track for best month since 2016\par     * China March PMI expands at the quickest pace in three\par months\par \par     By Nikhil Nainan\par     March 31 (Reuters) - Indonesia's rupiah slid to its weakest\par level in nearly five months on Wednesday as rising U.S. bond\par yields continued to sap appetite for one of the highest-yielding\par emerging markets.\par     Stocks in Jakarta and Kuala Lumpur fell\par around 1.5%, while neighbouring Manila was down 0.8%,\par with all three leadings declines across the continent.\par     Later on Wednesday U.S. President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joe Biden\f28 \uc2 \f2  is set to\par outline how he intends to pay for a $3 trillion to 4 trillion\par infrastructure plan that could further boost the U.S. recovery\par versus the world.\par     "The additional spending boost could further support the US\par dollar," Mizuho said in a note. \par     The dollar is on track for its best month since 2016\par compared with monthly declines set for many in Asia's emerging\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including the rupiah and ringgit. \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  said strong U.S. growth\par could help, but may also cause tighter financial conditions and\par trigger significant outflows from emerging countries.\par \par     Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor in pre-recorded remarks\par said the country has ample foreign reserves to ensure the rupiah\par is stable. He added that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does not hesitate to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to defend the rupiah.\par     Jakarta's stock market was also set for its worst month\par since September last year.\par     In Malaysia, stocks suffered their sharpest fall in nearly\par three months, falling as much as 1.8%. \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it expects Malaysia's economic growth\par in a range of 6% to 7.5% this year, compared with a 5.6%\par contraction - its worst since the Asian Financial Crisis in 1998\par - in 2020.\par     MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside of\par Japan, though up on Wednesday, was on track for\par its first loss in five months.    \par     China's manufacturing activity expanding at its quickest\par pace in three months in March did little to support the region.\par The yuan, however, edged higher, while Shanghai\par stocks fell 0.8%.\par     Singapore stocks also dipped, but are headed for\par their best month since November.\par     Eyes will also be on markets in India as COVID-19 cases rise\par sharply. The rupee weakened to a three week low on\par Tuesday.\par             \par     HIGHLIGHTS:\par     ** Glove makers \f28 \uc2 \f2 Top Glove Corp Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hartalega\par Holdings Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2  led declines in Malaysia\par     ** Indonesian 10-year benchmark yields rose 6.5 basis points\par to 6.859%\par     ** Asian countries scramble for vaccine supplies after India\par export curbs\par  Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0338 GMT\par  COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2      INDEX     STOCKS   STOCKS\par                           DAILY %  YTD %            DAILY %  YTD %\par 