\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Indonesia's rupiah hits 4-month low as U.S. bond yields rise\b0\par\par\uc2 518 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2021\par 05:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says they are ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Thai stocks rise as OPEC+ extends output cuts\par     * China sets above 6% growth target \par \par     March 5 (Reuters) - Indonesia's rupiah fell to a four-month\par low on Friday as rising U.S. bond yields sent the dollar higher\par and hit appetite for Asia's emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stock markets.\par     Stock markets in Asia suffered early falls following\par overnight comments by Federal Reserve Chair \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jerome Powell\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who \par reiterated that the Fed's policy stance as appropriate, even\par though he thought the recent bond sell-off was "notable".\par \par     By midday, Asian markets recovered some ground, with most\par standing less than half a percent lower, while Seoul was\par down 0.6%.\par     "Any fallout has been limited in Asia thus far thanks to\par China," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jeffrey Halley\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a senior market analyst for Asia\par Pacific at \f28 \uc2 \f2 OANDA\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par      China set a more than 6% growth target for 2021 at its\par parliamentary meeting, having skipped a target last year due to\par the pandemic.\par     Stocks in China eased off session lows and were down\par 0.3%, while the yuan was flat. \par     The Chinese government aimed to create more jobs in 2021\par than it did last year. \par     China's GDP expanded 2.3% last year, the only major economy\par to see growth, as it was the first to emerge from the pandemic.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it was ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the market to keep the weakening rupiah steady. The\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was down 0.3% on Friday.\par     Yields on Indonesian 10-year bonds rose 3.50\par basis points to 6.641%, while stocks dipped 0.1%.\par Indonesia has some of the highest-yielding debt in emerging\par markets. \par     In the Philippines, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said a spike in\par inflation to its fastest rate in 26 months was temporary,\par suggesting it was not in a rush to reverse its accommodative\par monetary policy stance.\par     Stocks and the peso were flat. \par         \par     HIGHLIGHTS:\par     ** Malaysia's 3-year benchmark yield up 11.4 basis points at\par 2.157%\par     ** Thai oil and gas producer \f28 \uc2 \f2 PTT Pcl\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 2.5% \par     ** Analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  say inflation will now start to rank\par higher on the priority list of central banks in emerging markets\par     \par   Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0344 GMT\par  COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YTD  INDEX    STOCKS   STOCKS\par                           DAILY %  %                DAILY %  YTD %\par  Japan                    +0.05    -4.34            -1.97    3.33\par  China                    -0.04    +0.85            -0.36    0.51\par  India                    +0.00    +0.32            0.00     7.86\par  Indonesia                -0.28    -1.82            -0.11    5.09\par  Malaysia                 -0.27    -1.13            0.19     -2.64\par  Philippines              +0.02    -1.13            -0.01    -3.61\par  S.Korea                  -0.27    -3.72            -0.62    5.26\par  Singapore                -0.01    -1.23            -0.23    5.77\par  Taiwan                   +1.33    +2.11            -0.19    7.76\par  Thailand                 -0.23    -1.51            0.48     6.35\par  \par  (Reporting by Nikhil Kurian Nainan in Bengaluru; Editing by\par Simon Cameron-Moore)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-3-5T05:50:27.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210305eh3500s0d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
