\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Indonesia stocks,  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  steady after virus-scarred week\b0\par\par\uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2020\par 10:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Indonesia stocks swing sharply through the session \par     * 10-year Indonesia bond yields rise to highest since\par late-July\par     * Graphic: Foreign flows into Asian stocks \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2ReJOMr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2ReJOMr}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  bullish on the rupee\par \par     Sept 11 (Reuters) - Indonesia's stock and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets\par touched multi-month lows on Friday as fresh COVID-19\par restrictions in Jakarta ate into hopes of an economic recovery,\par while overnight selling on Wall Street kept sentiment across\par Asia subdued.\par     Jakarta's main index rose 2%, after dropping nearly\par 3% early in the day, but was still on course for a weekly fall\par of around 5% that would be its worst performance since March. \par     The rupiah, the region's worst hit \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this\par year, slipped 0.4% and was heading for its biggest weekly loss\par in a month.\par     Ahead of the open, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said again it would\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot market to stabilise the rupiah, which\par backs one of Asia's most popular bond markets for foreign\par investors.\par     Even with the spate of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent days, the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has dropped 2% in the two weeks since investors were\par taken aback by a parliamentary panel's recommendations for\par changes to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Investors fear the proposed changes could reduce the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's independence at a time when the economy is struggling\par with rising COVID-19 cases with a fresh lockdown in Jakarta from\par Monday threatening to add further harm to its economy.\par     "Foreign investors were already concerned about proposed\par changes to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  law that could erode \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank\par Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2 's independence," said Khoon Goh, ANZ's head of Asia\par research. \par     "Now with the capital heading into lockdown, Indonesia's\par economic recovery is in doubt and this will see further investor\par caution, likely leading to near-term outflows."\par     Equity markets in the rest of emerging Asia found their\par footing late in the day as Shanghai shares rose, with\par Manila up 1% and stocks in Seoul erasing losses\par to trade flat. However, renewed selling of high-valued U.S. tech\par stocks overnight kept investors uneasy.\par     "With risk sentiment edgy, investors are likely to remain on\par the sidelines heading into the weekend," said Edward Ng, an\par Asian fixed income portfolio manager at Nikko Asset Management\par in Singapore.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were mostly higher against a weaker dollar.\par Malaysia's ringgit gained 0.2% after its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par held rates on Thursday in what some economists saw as a sign it\par will hold off on further cuts this year.\par     The Indian rupee was flat and stocks in Mumbai\par  edged higher as Indo-China tensions looked to be\par de-escalating.\par     The rupee is set to snap four straight weeks of gains, but\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 , buoyed by large foreign inflows in August, said\par it is bullish on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , pointing to moves by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve\par Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  to allow it to appreciate and an attractive carry\par trade volatility ratio.\par \par     HIGHLIGHTS: \par     ** Indonesian 10-year benchmark yields up 9.5 basis points\par to 6.996%\par     ** Top gainers in Jakarta include \f28 \uc2 \f2 Akbar Indo Makmur Stimec\par Tbk PT\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pollux Properti Indonesia Tbk PT\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par     ** Malaysia's 10-year benchmark yield up 2.5 basis points to\par 2.712%\u8203\'20\'0B\u8203\'20\'0B    \par            Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0706 GMT\par  COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2      INDEX    STOCKS   STOCKS\par                           DAILY %  YTD %           DAILY %  YTD %\par  Japan                      -0.03  +2.31              0.74   -1.06\par  China                      -0.04  +1.85              0.75    6.85\par  India                      -0.04  -2.86              0.11   -5.81\par  Indonesia                  -0.40  -6.72              2.19  -20.65\par  Malaysia                   +0.19  -1.56              0.13   -6.09\par  Philippines                +0.04  +4.30              1.11  -23.64\par  S.Korea                    -0.17  -2.57              0.01    9.06\par  Singapore                  +0.23  -1.62             -0.37  -22.96\par  Taiwan                     +0.84  +2.85             -0.12    5.66\par  Thailand                   -0.03  -4.47             -0.03  -18.32\par  \par  (Reporting by Nikhil Kurian Nainan in Bengaluru; Editing by\par Patrick Graham and Amy Caren Daniel)\f28 \par \