\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Indonesia's rupiah drops, stocks set for worst week since March\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2020\par 07:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Indonesia stocks swing sharply between positive and\par negative\par territory\par     * 10-year Indonesia bond yields rise to highest since\par late-July\par     * Graphic: Foreign flows into Asian stocks \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2ReJOMr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2ReJOMr}}}\uc2 \f2 \par \par     By Nikhil Nainan\par     Sept 11 (Reuters) - Indonesian shares swung wildly and the\par rupiah fell to its weakest since May on Friday as fresh COVID-19\par restrictions in Jakarta laid bare recovery hopes, while broader\par Asia was mixed after falls in Wall Street overnight.\par     After falling almost 3% early in the session, the Jakarta\par share market recovered some poise but was still on\par course for its worst week since March while the rupiah,\par by far the region's worst performer this year, lost nearly 1%.\par     Despite \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to protect the rupiah,\par which backs one of Asia's most popular bond markets for foreign\par investors, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has still dropped almost 2% in the two\par weeks since investors took fright at a parliamentary panel's\par recommendations for changes to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  law.\par     Investors fear the proposed changes could reduce the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's independence at a time when the economy is struggling\par with rising COVID-19 cases and the global fallout from the\par pandemic.\par     "Foreign investors were already concerned about proposed\par changes to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  law that could erode \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank\par Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2 's independence," said Khoon Goh, ANZ's head of Asia\par research. \par     "Now with the capital heading into lockdown, Indonesia's\par economic recovery is in doubt and this will see further investor\par caution, likely leading to near-term outflows."\par     Ahead of the open, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said again that it would\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot market to help stabilise the rupiah, a\par carry trade favourite for foreign investors, while the yield on\par 10-year bonds climbed 11 basis points to 7.014%, its highest\par since late July. \par     The bank holds a policy meeting next Thursday.\par     Other equity markets in emerging Asia were marginally lower,\par with South Korean stocks down the most, losing around\par 0.8%, taking a cue from the overnight selling of high-valued\par U.S. tech stocks.\par     "With risk sentiment edgy, investors are likely to remain on\par the sidelines heading into the weekend," said Edward Ng, an\par Asian fixed income portfolio manager at Nikko Asset Management\par in Singapore.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were mostly higher against a broadly weaker\par dollar. Malaysia's ringgit advanced 0.2% after its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held rates on Thursday in what some economists saw\par as a sign it will hold off with further cuts this year.\par \par \par     HIGHLIGHTS: \par     ** Indonesian 10-year benchmark yields up 11.3 basis points\par to 7.014%\par     ** In Jakarta: Top gainers include \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pollux Properti Indonesia\par Tbk PT\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rig Tenders Indonesia Tbk PT\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; top\par losers was \f28 \uc2 \f2 Acset Indonusa Tbk PT\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par     ** Malaysia's 10-year benchmark yield up 2.5 basis points at\par 2.712%    \par            Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0348 GMT\par  COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2      INDEX    STOCKS   STOCKS\par                           DAILY %  YTD %           DAILY %  YTD %\par  Japan                      -0.02  +2.32              0.30   -1.49\par  China                      -0.07  +1.82             -0.21    5.83\par  India                      +0.00  -2.82              0.00   -5.91\par  Indonesia                  -0.67  -6.97              0.75  -21.77\par  Malaysia                   +0.22  -1.54              0.59   -5.66\par  Philippines                +0.12  +4.39              0.95  -23.76\par  S.Korea                    -0.33  -2.73             -0.78    8.19\par  Singapore                  +0.19  -1.67             -0.49  -23.05\par  Taiwan                     +0.81  +2.82             -0.47    5.30\par  Thailand                   +0.06  -4.38              0.33  -18.02\par  \par  (Reporting by Nikhil Kurian Nainan in Bengaluru; Editing by\par Simon Cameron-Moore)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-11T06:05:09.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200911eg9b00jzh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
