\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Indonesia, Malaysia among Asia's top pct losers; rupiah slides again\b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2020\par 09:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in spot market\par     * Petronas units among losers in Malaysia as oil prices drop\par  \par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meets on Thursday \par     * Graphic: World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\uc2 \f2 \par \par     Sept 9 (Reuters) - Indonesian, Malaysian and Philippine\par stocks led declines in Asia's emerging markets on Wednesday, as\par a global sell-off in technology stocks helped the dollar find\par some support, which in turn weighed on regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Moving into afternoon trade, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were mostly lower\par with Indonesia's rupiah falling the most, which forced\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has fallen nearly 1.5% over the past week and\par lost another 0.5% on Wednesday, as concerns about government\par influence over its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dominated headlines.\par     In Malaysia, the listed units of state-run oil and gas group\par Petronas were among the top percentage losers, while in Jakarta,\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Medco Energi Internasional\f28 \uc2 \f2  plunged the most as oil\par prices fell below $40 a barrel on worries over demand.\par     Indonesia's main index fell 2% to a one-month low,\par while the Malaysian index dropped as much as 1.9% to its\par lowest since the end of June.\par     A slim majority of economists expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  to\par shave 25 basis points off its main rate on Thursday, bringing it\par to a record low of 1.5% to help offset the impact of the\par coronavirus crisis and jump start its economy.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has already slashed rates by 125 bps this\par year, but ING economist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Prakash Sakpal\f28 \uc2 \f2  argued that the ringgit's\par  4% rise against the dollar since June should give the\par bank more room for manoeuvre. \par     The ringgit dipped 0.2% against the dollar.\par     A top Indonesian minister told Reuters that the country must\par synchronise monetary and fiscal policies better to tackle the\par economic fallout of the pandemic, but said the government does\par not back a proposal for a monetary board to oversee \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank\par Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     The rupiah is suffering from concerns over the proposed\par changes to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  charter as well as selling of\par Indonesian bonds, which are among the region's favourite\par high-yielding carry trades for foreign investors, said Wei Liang\par Chang, a macro strategist at DBS.\par     The country's external debt was $408.6 billion in the second\par quarter, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bi.go.id/en/ruang-media/info-terbaru/Pages/Utang-Luar-Negeri-Indonesia-Triwulan-II-2020-Meningkat.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.bi.go.id/en/ruang-media/info-terbaru/Pages/Utang-Luar-Negeri-Indonesia-Triwulan-II-2020-Meningkat.aspx}}}\uc2 \f2 #\par  in August, adding up to 37.3% of gross domestic product.\par     A bi-weekly bond auction on Tuesday raised 22 trillion\par rupiah, but total bids fell to their lowest in more than four\par months.\par     \par     HIGHLIGHTS:\par     ** Indonesian 10-year benchmark yields up 2.4 basis points\par to 6.907%\u8203\'20\'0B\u8203\'20\'0B\par     ** Top percentage losers on Malaysia's benchmark index\par  were \f28 \uc2 \f2 Top Glove Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hartalega Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par \par     ** Malaysia's 3-year benchmark yield up 0.5 basis points to\par 1.859%    \par            Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0709 GMT\par  COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2      INDEX    STOCKS   STOCKS\par                           DAILY %  YTD %           DAILY %  YTD %\par  Japan                      +0.15  +2.61             -1.04   -2.64\par  China                      -0.03  +1.67             -1.82    6.75\par  India                      -0.09  -3.10             -0.88   -7.81\par  Indonesia                  -0.54  -6.47             -1.92  -18.35\par  Malaysia                   -0.19  -2.01             -1.60   -5.90\par  Philippines                -0.01  +4.14             -1.68  -24.09\par  S.Korea                    -0.23  -2.75             -1.09    8.11\par  Singapore                  -0.03  -1.93             -0.54  -22.70\par  Taiwan                     +0.70  +2.75             -0.43    5.10\par  Thailand                   +0.13  -4.71             -0.42  -18.45\par  \par  (Reporting by Nikhil Kurian Nainan in Bengaluru; Editing by Amy\par Caren Daniel)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-9T08:51:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200909eg9900u0l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\