\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Malaysia leads Asian stocks losses, rupiah slides again\b0\par\par\uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2020\par 06:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in spot market\par     * Petronas units among losers in Malaysia as oil prices drop\par  \par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meets on Thursday \par     * Graphic: World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\uc2 \f2 \par \par     By Nikhil Nainan\par     Sept 9 (Reuters) - Malaysian, Philippine and Indonesian\par stocks led falls in Asia's emerging stock markets on Wednesday,\par as a tech sector sell-off drove a wave of global selling and a\par drop in oil prices added to the pain for Malaysia's\par state-controlled oil companies. \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets across the region were more resilient,\par although Indonesia's rupiah again suffered after a week\par dominated by concerns about government influence over its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , forcing the bank to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again in markets.\par     In Kuala Lumpur, the listed units of state-run oil\par and gas group Petronas were among the top losers as oil prices\par fell below $40 a barrel for the first time since June on worries\par over demand.\par     The index fell as much as 1.9% to its lowest since the end\par of June.\par     With central banks across the region cutting interest rates\par to offset the impact of the coronavirus crisis, a slim majority\par of analysts expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  to shave another 25\par basis points off its main rate on Thursday, bringing it to a\par record low of 1.5%.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has already slashed rates by 125 bps this\par year but ING economist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Prakash Sakpal\f28 \uc2 \f2  argued that the ringgit's\par  4% rise against the dollar since June should give the\par bank more room for manoeuvre. \par     The ringgit dipped 0.2% against the dollar in morning trade.\par     The rupiah has fallen more than 1% in the past week\par and it was down another 0.5% on Wednesday, with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's head of monetary management telling Reuters it had\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to calm spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\par     Wei Liang Chang, a macro strategist at DBS, said the rupiah\par was continuing to suffer from concerns over proposed changes to\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  charter as well as selling of Indonesian bonds,\par among the region's favourite high-yielding carry trades for\par foreign investors.\par     A bi-weekly bond auction on Tuesday raised 22 trillion\par rupiah, but the total bids fell to their lowest in more than\par four months.\par     A top Indonesian minister told Reuters that the country must\par synchronise monetary and fiscal policies better to tackle\par economic pressures caused by the pandemic, although the\par government does not back a proposal for a monetary board to\par oversee \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     \par     HIGHLIGHTS:\par     ** Top losers on Malaysia's benchmark index were \f28 \uc2 \f2 Top\par Glove Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 IHH Healthcare\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par     ** Indonesian 10-year benchmark yields are up 3 basis points\par to 6.912%\par     ** Malaysia's 3-year benchmark yield is up 0.5 basis points\par to 1.859%  \par            Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0353 GMT\par  COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2      INDEX    STOCKS   STOCKS\par                           DAILY %  YTD %           DAILY %  YTD %\par  Japan                      +0.06  +2.51             -1.48   -3.07\par  China                      -0.04  +1.66             -1.07    7.57\par  India                      +0.00  -3.01             -0.87   -7.81\par  Indonesia                  -0.51  -6.44             -1.20  -17.75\par  Malaysia                   -0.17  -1.99             -1.29   -5.60\par  Philippines                +0.10  +4.26             -1.37  -23.85\par  S.Korea                    -0.28  -2.80             -0.78    8.44\par  Singapore                  -0.08  -1.98             -0.59  -22.74\par  Taiwan                     +0.71  +2.76             -0.92    4.58\par  Thailand                   +0.13  -4.71             -0.88  -18.83\par  \par \par  (Reporting by Nikhil Kurian Nainan in Bengaluru; Editing by Kim\par Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-9T05:58:42.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200909eg9900ho5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
