\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Indonesia markets sink as Chinese moves hit Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2020\par 09:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Graphic: World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     * Graphic: Foreign flows into Asian stocks \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3f2vwbA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3f2vwbA}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     * Baht tracking worst day in two weeks\par     * Rupiah down for third straight day\par     * Malaysia stocks slip 1%\par \par     By Shashwat Awasthi\par     Sept 3 (Reuters) - Indonesia's rupiah eased further and its stocks tumbled almost 1% on\par Thursday as worries over growth, its policy response and planned changes that analysts worry may\par threaten the independence of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  all dented sentiment.\par     The falls came on a rollercoaster morning for Asian shares, with stock markets across the\par region lower as a report that China was planning sweeping policy changes to its semiconductor\par industry to fight U.S. restrictions outweighed a batch of upbeat data from China.\par     Stocks in Malaysia slipped 0.9%, while Singapore fell 0.8%.\par     The rupiah gave up another 0.3%, taking weekly losses in South East Asia's weakest\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year to more than 1%, even after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BI) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stabilize the\par market on Wednesday.\par     Proposals earlier this week for a new monetary policy framework that would shake up BI's\par decision making and potentially give ministers a say on interest rates have shaken investors at\par a time when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is already aiding government fiscal stimulus efforts to back-stop\par an emergency bond issuance scheme.\par     Anthony Kevin, macro economist at Mirae Asset Sekuritas Indonesia, called the timing of the\par proposals "very unfortunate" and said the widening of BI's mandate to include supporting\par economic growth and jobs might disrupt the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's focus.\par     "As the discussion over the draft bill is still in a preliminary stage, we expect the\par pressures on rupiah to continue in the short to medium term," he said.\par     Thailand's baht, hit by the sudden resignation of the finance minister this week,\par also weakened for the second straight day, falling 0.6%.\par     Most other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded flat to slightly lower as the U.S. dollar bounced,\par although a Reuters poll showed bullish bets on Asian units had risen after four straight months\par of declines in the greenback.\par     Philippine shares snapped a five-day losing streak and advanced 0.6% as the country's\par unemployment rate dropped in July from a record high three months ago.\par             \par     HIGHLIGHTS:\par     \par     ** Indonesian 10-year benchmark yields are up 3.8 basis points at 6.946%\par     ** Top losers on the Jakarta stock index include Megapolitan Developments\par down 7%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alfa Energi Investama\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 6.85% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Langgeng Makmur\f28 \uc2 \f2  Industri down\par 6.74%\par     ** Top losers on FTSE \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bursa Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Kl Index include Hartalega down\par 7.16%, Genting down 2.2% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 IOI Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 1.99%\par     \par     \par         Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0652 GMT                                 \par    COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DAILY %    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YTD %    INDEX     STOCKS DAILY %   STOCKS YTD %\par     Japan                     -0.10       +2.18                     0.94            -0.81\par     China                     -0.05       +1.78                    -0.77            10.77\par     India                     -0.48       -2.73                     0.05            -5.16\par   Indonesia                   -0.30       -6.12                    -0.96           -16.49\par   Malaysia                    -0.05       -1.35                    -0.94            -4.13\par  Philippines                  -0.04       +4.28                     0.60           -26.13\par    S.Korea                    -0.24       -2.68                     1.33            9.02\par   Singapore                   -0.14       -1.46                    -0.75           -21.78\par    Taiwan                     +0.69       +2.67                     0.46            6.34\par   Thailand                    -0.60       -4.78                     0.06           -16.66\par  \par  (Reporting by Shashwat Awasthi and Arpit Nayak in Bengaluru; Editing by Patrick Graham and Amy\par Caren Daniel)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-3T08:45:15.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200903eg9301249\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
