\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Rupiah, baht resume decline; Thai stocks cheer stimulus\b0\par\par\uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2020\par 07:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Graphic: World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     * Graphic: Foreign flows into Asian stocks \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3f2vwbA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3f2vwbA}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     * Baht hits one-week low\par     * Rupiah lower for third straight day\par     * Malaysia stocks lose almost 1%\par \par     By Shashwat Awasthi\par     Sept 3 (Reuters) - Indonesia's rupiah eased on Thursday as doubts about the fiscal and\par monetary policy mix and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's independence weighed on sentiment, while additional\par fiscal stimulus in Thailand supported Bangkok stocks for a second day.\par     A bounce in the U.S. dollar kept gains among other emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in check, with\par many trading flat to marginally higher on the day.\par     Thailand's baht, hit by the sudden resignation of the finance minister this week,\par weakened for the second straight day and fell 0.3%.\par     The Indonesian rupiah gave up another 0.4%, taking weekly losses in South East Asia's\par most laggard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year to more than 1%, even after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's (BI) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par to stabilize the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday.\par     Proposals earlier this week for a new monetary policy framework that would shake up \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank\par Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2 's decision making and potentially give ministers a say on interest rates have shaken\par investors at a time when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is already aiding government fiscal stimulus efforts\par to back-stop an emergency bond issuance scheme.\par     Indonesia's economy has been among the hardest hit by the coronavirus crisis this year. \par     "We could see modest bouts of associated IDR softening on further developments in the near\par term," Maybank analysts said in a note.\par     "One key area markets would be scrutinizing is the confluence between ceding of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par independence and the potential for larger extent of debt monetization by BI."\par     The country's main stock index slipped 0.7%, and analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 OCBC Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  cited\par confusion about whether debt monetization was a one-off or not as a possible hindrance.\par     Stocks in Thailand added 0.5% and have climbed more than 1% in the last two days as\par $2.2 billion in cash handouts and job measures announced Wednesday boosted risk taking.\par \par     Data on Thursday also showed core inflation in August rose in line with analysts' estimates.\par \par     South Korean shares jumped almost 1.5% amid a rally in Asian equities after a\par sustained recovery in China's services sector and the prospect of additional U.S. stimulus to\par support the economy.\par     Malaysia's main index slid 0.9%, however, giving up almost all the gains it had\par recorded in the previous session.\par     \par     \par     HIGHLIGHTS\par     \par     ** Indonesian 10-year benchmark yields are up 4.5 basis points at 6.953%\par     ** Top losers on the Jakarta stock index include Megapolitan Developments\par down 7%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alfa Energi Investama\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 6.85% and Sunson Textile Manufacturer\par down 6.8%\par     ** Top losers on FTSE \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bursa Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Kl Index include Hartalega Holdings\par down 5.21%, Tenaga Nasional down 1.78% and Maxis down 1.76%\par     \par     \par    Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0419 GMT                                  \par    COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DAILY %    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YTD %    INDEX   STOCKS DAILY %  STOCKS YTD %\par     Japan                    -0.09       +2.19                   1.28          -0.47\par     China                    +0.05       +1.89                  -0.06          11.56\par     India                    +0.00       -2.25                   0.13          -5.09\par   Indonesia                  -0.20       -6.03                  -0.59          -16.18\par   Malaysia                   +0.05       -1.26                  -0.86          -4.05\par  Philippines                 +0.03       +4.35                   0.12          -26.49\par    S.Korea                   -0.12       -2.56                   1.45           9.14\par   Singapore                  -0.01       -1.34                  -0.63          -21.68\par    Taiwan                    +0.63       +2.60                   0.54           6.43\par   Thailand                   -0.29       -4.47                   0.46          -16.32\par  \par  (Reporting by Shashwat Awasthi in Bengaluru; Editing by Patrick Graham and Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-3T06:09:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200903eg9300j51\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
