\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Rupiah, baht clobbered as policy uncertainty weighs\b0\par\par\uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2020\par 07:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Graphic: World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     * Graphic: Foreign flows into Asian stocks \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3f2vwbA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3f2vwbA}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     * Rupiah set for worst day in over five months\par     * Philippine stocks fall for fifth straight day\par \par     By Shashwat Awasthi\par     Sept 2 (Reuters) - Indonesia's rupiah was the biggest mover\par among emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday, weakening 1.5%\par after officials failed to quell concerns that a proposed new\par framework for monetary policy-making could undermine the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's independence.\par     Thailand's baht also slipped on the abrupt resignation of\par the country's newly appointed finance minister.\par     Dogged by worries over Indonesia's economic growth, debt and\par a difficult policy mix, the rupiah was set for its worst\par day since March 23, prompting \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par smooth the exchange rate volatility. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also\par affirmed its "accommodative" policy stance.\par \par     An advisory panel to the government on Monday proposed the\par biggest shake-up in monetary policy decision-making in two\par decades, including a call for ministers to be given voting\par rights at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia (BI) \f28 \uc2 \f2 policy meetings.\par     President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joko Widodo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's assurances on Tuesday that BI would\par remain independent did little to lower concerns among foreign\par investors.\par     "Such a move would undermine BI's independence and could\par open the door to successive rounds of debt monetization,\par something that would rattle investor confidence and exert\par additional depreciation pressure on the IDR," Dutch bank ING\par said in a note.\par     Jakarta stocks fell 0.2% while the rupiah, already\par South East Asia's worst performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this year, saw its\par year-to-date losses swell over 6%.\par     In Thailand, the baht gave up 0.5% as the\par resignation of finance minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Predee Daochai\f28 \uc2 \f2  after less than a\par month in the job sparked uncertainty while the government tries\par to support the tourism-reliant economy.\par \par     Daochai was appointed by Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Prayuth Chan-ocha\f28 \uc2 \f2  to\par manage Southeast Asia's second-largest economy through the\par coronavirus crisis after a slew of cabinet resignations in July\par had paved the way for a major reshuffle.\par     Daochai's decision to step down "could create the impression\par that the cabinet may have a stability issue," analysts at\par Bangkok-based Phillip Securities said.\par     They added that finding a successor might be difficult as\par many prospective candidates approached last month had turned\par down the role and that this could delay state investments and\par stimulus measures.\par     Thailand's deputy prime minister on Wednesday said, however,\par that the government does not expect any big impact on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par from the resignation.\par     \par     HIGHLIGHTS:\par     \par     ** Indonesian 10-year benchmark yields are up 7.4 basis\par points at 6.938%\par     ** In the Philippines, top index losers are Ayala Land\par  down 3.18%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Universal Robina\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 2.46% and\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of the Philippine Islands\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 2.3%\par     ** Top losers on the Singapore STI include \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jardine\par Matheson\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 1.65%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capitaland Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  down\par 1.43% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 DBS Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 0.96%\par \par     \par     Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0354 GMT                                   \par    COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DAILY %     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YTD %     INDEX   STOCKS DAILY %  STOCKS YTD %\par     Japan                    -0.08        +2.42                    0.24          -1.96\par     China                    -0.07        +1.92                   -0.39          11.38\par     India                    0.00         -2.03                    0.07          -5.67\par   Indonesia                  -1.52        -6.15                   -0.04          -15.73\par   Malaysia                   -0.17        -1.40                    0.50          -3.76\par  Philippines                 +0.02        +4.24                   -0.62          -26.26\par    S.Korea                   -0.31        -2.55                   -0.21           6.68\par   Singapore                  +0.02        -1.22                   -0.44          -21.58\par    Taiwan                    +0.59        +2.58                   -0.32           5.55\par   Thailand                   -0.58        -4.26                    0.22          -17.18\par  \par  (Reporting by Shashwat Awasthi in Bengaluru)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-2T06:23:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200902eg9200kwt\p