\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Southeast Asia shares off lows as U.S. futures rise, virus fears remain\b0\par\par\uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2020\par 12:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rupiah regains ground after c.bank\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Thai shares end four weeks of gains \par     * U.S. stock futures up between 1.5% to 2.2%  \par \par     June 12 (Reuters) - Stock markets in Southeast Asia\par recovered from their session lows on Friday as U.S. futures\par rose, but fears of a new wave of coronavirus infections dampened\par hopes of a global economic recovery as countries emerge from\par their lockdowns.\par     Singapore and Kuala Lampur both ended 0.7%\par lower, rallying well off lows after losing more than 3% earlier.\par     Asian markets had taken their early bearish leads from Wall\par Street, where the three major U.S. stock indexes fell more than\par 5% on Thursday, posting their worst day since mid-March.\par     "Certainly not going to judge it today as it's Friday and\par the markets will try to settle in around current ranges and take\par a pause to reassess next week," Stephen Innes, the chief global\par markets strategist at AxiCorp said.\par     Among emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Indonesia's rupiah\par faced the brunt of the sell-off, ending 0.7% weaker against the\par dollar in a week that saw it drop 1.4% as the number of domestic\par coronavirus infections spiked to around a 1,000 a day.\par \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  did regain some ground from a 1.5% drop earlier\par after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot and domestic\par non-deliverable forward markets. \par     In Jakarta, equity markets ended higher after losing\par as much as 2.9% earlier. It marked the first weekly loss in\par three, a sign that the recent rally may have been overdone.\par     A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  survey on Friday showed consumer confidence\par tumbling to its lowest in 15 years in May. \par     Thai shares plunged 3.7% over the week, ending a\par four-week winning run even as the country plans to lift a\par national curfew and ease restrictions further next week after\par largely bringing local infections under control.\par     But with tourism a big contributor to growth and with bars\par to remain closed, there still remains some way to go. The baht\par  fell 0.2% to 30.98 per dollar. \par     Like China and much of the developed world, Southeast Asian\par countries have been steadily easing lockdown measures that have\par hammered their economies and are hoping for a swift reboot in\par the weeks ahead.\par     Philippine financial markets were closed for a public\par holiday.    \par         Change on the day at   0945 GMT             \par  COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DAILY %  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YTD %  STOCKS DAILY %  STOCKS YTD %\par  Japan        -0.56       +1.08     -0.75           -5.71\par  China        -0.18       -1.60     -0.04           -4.27\par  India        -0.08       -5.88     0.50            -18.22\par  Indonesia    -0.71       -1.21     0.53            -22.53\par  Malaysia     -0.42       -4.13     -0.72           -2.69\par  S.Korea      -0.61       -3.94     -2.04           -2.97\par  Singapore    +0.24       -3.25     -0.72           -16.70\par  Taiwan       -0.10       +1.16     -0.92           -4.73\par  Thailand     -0.16       -3.45     -1.02           -12.49\par  \par  (Reporting by Nikhil Kurian Nainan in Bengaluru; Editing by\pa