\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2020\par 11:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0927 GMT - Increased \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by emerging markets could be contributing to the euro's gains against the dollar, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. There are signs emerging markets are stepping up \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to contain the recent strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Thailand, South Korea and Malaysia adding to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, \uc2 MUFG\uc2 's \uc2 Derek Halpenny\uc2  says. "In order to rebalance these USD purchases, sales of USD for EUR tends to be the most common rebalancing flow given EUR is the second largest reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  held according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Composition of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 IMF\uc2  data." EUR/USD rises 0.2% to 1.1362 after hitting a five-day high of 1.1377 earlier, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0906 GMT - Sterling could weaken in coming months on growing worries the U.K. and EU will fail to agree a trade deal, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The U.K. and EU should agree an extension to the Brexit transition period beyond the end of 2020 given that the two sides have a "strong interest" in reaching a deal amid an "already difficult economic situation" caused by coronavirus, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's Thu \uc2 Lan Nguyen\uc2  says. However, the probability of a no-deal Brexit has "increased significantly," she says. "For this reason we are forecasting a weaker pound in the short term and only a very moderate recovery later in the year." \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  sees EUR/GBP rising to 0.90 by September from 0.8905 currently and GBP/USD falling to 1.24 from 1.2772. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0851 GMT - The \uc2 U.K. Debt Management Office\uc2  's planned auction of GBP900 million in 0.125% November 2036 index-linked gilts should go well given the small size of the transaction, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  . "On balance the bond does not stand out as particularly attractive on any metric, although given the small size of the auction...there should be few problems with take down of the bond," says the Canadian bank. The size of the deal, GBP900 million, is small compared to an intended average of GBP1-1.5 billion for a linker gilt with a tenor of over 10 years, as per the April's DMO remit revision, it says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT - The decline in Italian unemployment will soon reverse, says Jack Allen-Reynolds, senior Europe economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . While the unemployment rate has risen in almost every eurozone country, in Italy it fell from 9.1% in February to 6.3% in April. "As the crisis in Italy escalated, record numbers of disillusioned job seekers dropped out of the labour force, causing the unemployment rate to fall," the economist explains. Now that the lockdown is being lifted and activity is recovering, many of those people should return to the labour force, he says. "So an increase in the unemployment rate in the coming months could, paradoxically, be a sign that the economy is returning to some sense of normality," Allen-Reynolds says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT - Economic activity in the U.K. doesn't appear to be collapsing further but signs of improvement are scant, says \uc2 Peter Dixon\uc2  , U.K. economist at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . Real-time data, such as electricity generation and travel data, suggest that activity in May was close to the weak levels seen in April, and the indicators for early June are not promising, he says. Data suggests that retail activity remains 38% below its pre-coronavirus average, electricity generation is down 10% compared with the same period a year ago, the number of people going to their workplace is almost 50% below pre-crisis levels, and less than 7% of children are attending school. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT - Overnight stays in German accommodation establishments had the biggest year-on-year monthly fall in April since the metric was first recorded in 1992, statistics office Destatis says. In April there were 4.3 million overnight stays by visitors from Germany and abroad in accommodation establishments, a fall of 89% from a year earlier, according to Destatis' provisional results. The reason for the decline was the ban on accommodation for travelers that had been in effect since mid-March due to the coronavirus, Destatis says. Overnight stays by foreign travelers fell 93%, while the number of domestic guests declined 88.5% year-on-year. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0827 GMT - Brent crude oil is down 2% at $40.39 a barrel and WTI futures are down 2.5% at $37.97 a barrel, more than reversing Tuesday's gains. DNB's Helge Martinsen cites the build in U.S. inventories reported late Tuesday as being behind the slip. Investors and analysts have grown concerned in recent weeks that the rebound in oil prices will prompt some producers to ramp up production before the market has burned through the vast oil glut accrued during the coronavirus lockdowns. "As opportunistic buying eases and a considerable inventory cushion on both the crude and products side remains, strict OPEC+ compliance, at least in the next few months, and smooth demand recovery remain crucial to maintaining recent price gains," \uc2 JBC Energy\uc2  says in a note. \uc2 Energy Information Administration\uc2  data is due out later Wednesday. (david.hodari@wsj.com ; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 0810 GMT The September Bund future contract, currently trading at 173.40, is under pressure and stands below its 50-period moving average at 173.60. Moreover, the 30-min RSI remains within its selling area, between 50 and 30, and confirms the bearish bias. Further weakness is therefore expected toward horizontal support at 173.10 at first and toward June 8 bottom at 172.79 in extension. A third target is set at June 5 low at 172.57. Only a rebound above horizontal resistance at 173.85 would turn the outlook to bullish with a first alternative target set at horizontal resistance and overlap at 174.30 and a second one set at horizontal resistance at 174.75 in extension. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 GMT - Gold prices are up ahead of the conclusion of a Federal Reserve meeting. The precious metal rallied about $20 on Tuesday as stocks reversed some of their recent gains as investors took profits and awaited the FOMC meeting for clues on the state of the economy. COMEX futures are up 0.2% at $1,724.80 a troy ounce. Little policy surprises are anticipated from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's meeting but how the Fed characterizes the state of the U.S. economy after jobs data last week proved surprisingly strong will be key for risk assets, and therefore safe-haven gold, analysts said. "A further rotation to safety could continue to support gold prices today into the FOMC and beyond," \uc2 OANDA\uc2  says. (william.horner@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0725 GMT Currently trading at $1.1355, the Euro is on the upside and is supported by its rising 50-period moving average at $1.1318 on a 30-minute chart. From a technical point of view, the 14-period RSI stands within its buying area between 50 and 70 and confirms the bullish bias. As a consequence, as long as $1.1315 holds as horizontal support and overlap, further rise is expected toward June 5 top at $1.185 and toward horizontal resistance at $1.1400 in extension. A third target is set at horizontal resistance at $1.1425. Only a break below strong horizontal support at $1.1315 would invalidate this bullish view and favour a down move toward horizontal support at $1.1280 and even toward $1.1260 in extension. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0723 GMT - Austria's 'Aa1' credit rating with stable outlook reflects the country's diversified and competitive economy, strong institutions and high debt affordability, says \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  . It points to relatively weak trend growth and elevated debt levels compared with peers as Austria's main credit challenges, the ratings firm says in a new annual report. "Although the coronavirus pandemic will lead to at least a temporary deterioration in economic and fiscal metrics, we expect that the improvement in key credit metrics will resume once the shock abates," says Heiko Peters, a \uc2 Moody's\uc2  assistant vice president and analyst, and co-author of the report. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0719 GMT - The dollar index falls 0.1% to 96.2000, having earlier reached a 90-day low of 96.1130, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . But \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to be moved much by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  's latest policy decision or U.S. inflation data later Wednesday. "The Fed is set to reaffirm its forward guidance and its commitment to do whatever is needed to help the U.S. economic recovery, while restating its reluctance about considering negative policy rates," \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  analysts say. "If this is the outcome, we do not think the USD will be particularly hit," they say. U.S. inflation figures at 1230 GMT are "unlikely to have any major impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," they say. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 10, 2020 05:27 ET (09:27 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200610eg6a0013u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
