\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Most  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  set for weekly losses as economic pain from pandemic deepens\b0\par\par\uc2 670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2020\par 09:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Indian rupee leads losses in the region\par     * Ringgit pares gains as oil prices fall\par     * Philippine peso set for weekly gain of 1%\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Shriya Ramakrishnan\par     April 3 (Reuters) - Most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell on Friday and\par were on course to record weekly losses, as investors latched on\par to the safety of the U.S. dollar due to the worsening economic\par impact from the coronavirus pandemic.\par     While governments and central banks have stepped in with\par stimulus measures, enforced lockdowns and flooded markets with\par liquidity, risk appetite remains subdued due to a continued rise\par in cases of the flu-like illness.\par     Data on Thursday showed global coronavirus cases surpassed\par one million with more than 52,000 deaths, while weekly jobless\par claims shot to a record high of more than 6 million last week in\par the United States.\par     The South Korean won and Singapore dollar dipped\par 0.2% each, and were on track to record a weekly loss of 1.7% and\par 0.4%, respectively. \par     The Malaysian ringgit pared early gains to trade flat\par as oil prices reversed course following their biggest one-day\par gains in the previous session.\par     Fluctuations in crude prices had an impact on the ringgit,\par as Malaysia is a net exporter of the commodity. \par     The Indonesian rupiah was little changed, while the\par onshore Chinese yuan dropped up to 0.2%, led by the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weakest guidance since the 2008 global financial\par crisis.\par      Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves dropped by some $9\par billion in March, as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  intensified market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to manage the depreciation in the rupiah amid\par capital outflows.\par     Meanwhile, the Philippine peso gained 0.2% and was\par set to be the top performer in the region this week with a\par weekly gain of 1%.\par     "The Philippines has relatively more monetary policy space,\par both conventional and non-conventional, compared to some of its\par regional peers, while still boasting of positive real interest\par rates," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Han Tan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a market analyst at FXTM said. \par     "Having adequate buffers, both on the monetary and fiscal\par sides, can shore up investor confidence that policymakers are\par able to mitigate some of the negative impact of COVID-19." \par     Financial markets in Taiwan were closed for a holiday.\par     \par     INDIAN RUPEE\par     The Indian rupee slipped as much as 0.7% to 76.085\par against the dollar, two days after resuming trade. \par     A 21-day nationwide lockdown to curb coronavirus from\par spreading has raised fears of a damaging impact on the\par already-slowing economy.\par     Foreigners sold $8.13 billion worth of Indian bonds in\par March, the biggest monthly outflow since at least Jan. 2002.\par     A Reuters poll also showed that the rupee will probably cut\par some of its losses in the next year, but it's likely to stay\par weak along with other emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  until the\par coronavirus pandemic subsides.\par \par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     \par   Change on the day at            