\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Indonesia readying further measures to stabilise markets\b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 06:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, details on recent measures)\par \par \uc2 By Tabita Diela\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, March 10 (Reuters) - Indonesia Finance Minister \uc2 Sri Mulyani Indrawati\uc2  said on Tuesday that authorities are prepared to use all the measures they implemented during the 2008 global financial crisis to stabilise financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The options include buying back government bonds, Indrawati told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's high yielding assets are among the most attractive in emerging markets but with foreigners accounting for around 38% of holdings in government bonds, they are vulnerable to outflows.\par \par \uc2 "We will guard the market mechanism so its integrity remains intact, while preventing excessive profit taking and excessive speculation in this abnormal situation," she said.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  is buying government bonds from the secondary market, and we will collaborate in this," she added.\par \par \uc2 The minister said the recent dive in global markets due to plunging oil prices and coronavirus fears had been "extraordinary" and said the government will move to prevent it from hurting Indonesia's economy.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's main stock index on Monday posted its biggest single day drop since 2011 amid a global sell-off, bringing the year-to-date decline to 18.5% and triggered market measures by the Financial Service Authority and the \uc2 Indonesia Stock Exchange\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The exchange, starting Tuesday, will stop stock price declines at 10%, compared with a previous range of 20%-35%, while the Financial Services Authority will allow companies to buy back shares without shareholder approval.\par \par \uc2 Twelve listed state companies are planning to buy back up to $560 million of shares following the measure, a State-Owned Enterprises Ministry official said.\par \par \uc2 The main stock index on Tuesday clawed back some of its losses as Asian markets stabilised, gaining 2.3% by the midday break, while the rupiah rose 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 A deputy governor of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  told Reuters the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is intensifying its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in bonds, domestic non-deliverable and spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange markets to support the market.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Indrawati said the government will continue to monitor whether there is a need for more support should the spread of coronavirus be prolonged.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia late last month unveiled a near $750 million stimulus package to battle the impact of the virus outbreak to the economy.\par \par \uc2 "We don't want to exhaust our ammunition too early. We need to maintain our abilities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  should this condition is prolonged," she said. ( Additional reporting and writing by Fransiska Nangoy; Editing by Ed Davies and Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-3-10T06:51:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200310eg3a00nud\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
